        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Deck Sealing Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck sealant is cheap, quick, and easy to get wrong in ways that only become visible six months later. These four account for most of it.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a Dirty Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealant does not clean, hide, or fix — it locks in whatever is on the boards. Mildew sealed under a clear coat keeps growing, and grey weathered fibre sealed in stays grey. Clean and brighten first and let the deck dry properly, or you are preserving the problem for another two years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-Applying on Dense Boards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hardwoods and older, tight-grained decking absorb far less than the label assumes. Sealer that cannot soak in pools on the surface, stays tacky for days, and dries to a sticky, dirt-attracting film. Apply thin, and back-wipe any excess that has not absorbed within about fifteen minutes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Brand-New Pressure-Treated Lumber Immediately</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New treated lumber is saturated with treatment solution and will not accept sealer until it has dried out, which usually takes several months. Test with a few drops of water — if it beads, the board is not ready. Sealing early gives a coat that never penetrates and flakes off within a season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting for Visible Failure to Re-Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                By the time a deck looks grey and dry it has already taken UV and moisture damage that a fresh coat cannot undo. Re-seal on the interval, judged by whether water still beads on the boards, rather than waiting until the deck looks bad enough to demand it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16 text-center bg-blue-50 p-12 rounded-3xl border border-blue-100 shadow-inner">
          <h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Ready to Protect Your Deck?</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-8 max-w-xl mx-auto">
